  "text": "Neither solution holds up, so I'm choosing NONE, with low confidence in that judgment.\n\n**Solution 1 (d_c = 5)** rests on the ratio (d−1)/4 = 1, where (d−1) is the branching number and 1/4 is taken as the squared split weight. The argument never explains why the second-moment growth rate of the branching walk should set the L² cutoff threshold, and the \"Kesten–Stigum\" label is applied without a real connection to the averaging dynamics. The threshold is asserted rather than derived.\n\n**Solution 2 (d_c = 3)** is also heuristic. The \"noise cascade\" and the coefficients Q_jk are defined but never shown to produce a phase transition. The claim that d = 3 is special is supported only by the spectral gap being small there, which does not show that the mechanism fails for d ≥ 4. Its covariance equation and the variance bound are not used to derive the threshold either.\n\nI can't verify the correct value from memory. I'd expect the answer to come from a specific paper on L²-cutoff for the averaging process on random regular graphs, and neither solution reproduces a derivation that would single out its value. That makes both answers unsupported, though I can't rule out that one of them matches the published result.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
